The O3 lesson is at `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`. It is 30 minutes and about 4,700 words (measured), with two figures.

It opens with the big picture: text can live in three places, the window, saved notes and retrieval. A short recall of the agent loop follows. The sections then cover:
- tokens and the context window;
- why every step re-sends the whole history;
- a worked four-step makerspace example with token arithmetic;
- context rot, clearing old tool results and compaction;
- notes that survive between sessions;
- finding the right manual page (pre-retrieval, just-in-time, keyword versus embedding search).

Practice is three faded tasks, one on the token arithmetic, one on choosing a retrieval style, and a paper sketch of what the assistant keeps in the window, saves and retrieves.

The review came back REVISE and I fixed all but one finding. Retrieval got a fuller explanation and a task of its own. I removed the duplicated calculation and a redundant table, and corrected an arithmetic error in the clearing example and a word-count slip. The section times now sum to 30. The one I left open is that the context-rot and retrieval sections still run about 660 to 700 words each, which is long for how you like to read. If you want those cut, say so.

The token counts are invented for teaching and are labelled that way. Sources are Anthropic's context-engineering article and the Claude context-window docs.

You can ask me anything about the lesson in the chat, and a quiz is ready when you want one (ask with `/quiz`). I also wrote `review-records/0001-teach.md` and updated `NOTES.md`.

One question: should I split the two long sections into shorter chunks with a check between them?
